Mr. Albert:

For more than a year I have written to my government about its treatment 
toward me, and it has largely been silent, except when it sent police to 
my door to tell me I was not in trouble. And when I requested the public 
records to find out what was done with my letters, I was met with every 
plain act a government can engage in to prevent me from seeing those 
records. That is not how public records work, and I am asking you to do 
something about that.

Please see the attached documents.

--
*Robert Samuel White*
Post Office Box 334, Reedsport, Oregon, 97467
rswfire.com <https://rswfire.com/>
oprdvolunteerabuse.org <https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/>
autonomyrealms.com <https://autonomyrealms.com/>


On 10/13/26 13:43, PRC PublicRecordsAdvocate * PRA wrote:
>
> Hi Mr. White,
>
> Because of the way the public records law is written, the Oregon 
> Attorney General does not have the authority to adjudicate appeals 
> related to actions taken by an elected state official, including the 
> governor. Those matters may only be appealed to the circuit court.
>
> Our office also does not have the authority to compel the governor’s 
> office to take action. We can, however, provide guidance and advice on 
> the application of the public records law to individual requests.
>
> I can take a look at the matter and share my thoughts with you on 
> them. If the situation calls for it, I can share them with the 
> governor’s office as well.
>
> To begin, please provide me with a copy of your request, including 
> wherever you made your public interest fee waiver argument, and the 
> response from the governor’s office denying the request.
>
> Thanks,
>
> Todd
>
> Todd Albert
>
> Public Records Advocate
>
